Chapter: Passes

 Runs the IR-to-IR passes as an ordered pipeline. The pipeline is
 data — a list of pass names — so a pass can be switched off,
 reordered, or ablated by editing one list instead of rethreading a
 call chain. Dispatch is deliberately by name rather than through
 function-valued record fields: one list names the passes, one
 function maps a name to its transform, and nothing else has to
 exist for a pass to be registered.

Section: Pass Result

 A pass returns its chapter and whatever it has to say about it. The
 rewriters have nothing to say and return the empty list; the checker
 rewrites nothing and returns everything it found. One shape carries
 both, because a pipeline with two kinds of pass in it is a pipeline
 with two ways to be wired wrong.

  IRPassResult = record {
   chapter : IRChapter,
   violations : List Text,
   infos : List Text
  }

Section: Pass Registry

 An unknown name is the identity transform. A diagnostic for a
 misspelled pass name belongs to the configuration surface and
 arrives with it; until then the pipeline must never turn a bad
 name into a changed program.

 `ir-check` is a checker in a rewriter's clothing. It is registered
 like any other pass so it can be placed anywhere — and more than once
 — in the pipeline: `passes=fold-constants,ir-check,inline-leaf-calls,
 ir-check` brackets each rewriter with a validation of what it
 produced. It returns the chapter unchanged and reports through the
 violation channel instead. It is deliberately NOT in
 `default-ir-pipeline`: the shipping compiler pays nothing for a
 validator it never runs, and the validator is wanted exactly when a
 pass is under suspicion.

 `occ-report` is the same shape, but on the OTHER channel: an analysis
 wearing a rewriter's clothing. It runs the occurrence analyser
 (`occ-report-chapter`) over every definition and returns the chapter
 UNCHANGED, reporting each definition's per-binder classifications — one
 `OCC <def>: name=Class, name=Class` line per definition — as **infos,
 not violations**. That distinction is the whole reason the result
 carries two lists. `ir-check`'s findings are errors: malformed IR means
 the binary is not the program, so the compile halts. `occ-report`'s
 findings are information about well-formed IR, so they ride the info
 channel, do not halt, and never claim to be an error — running
 `-Passes occ-report` reports the classifications AND still emits a
 binary. It exists so the analyser can be driven over real lowered IR
 and its answers read from the compile log, before any pass consumes
 them. Like `ir-check` it is NOT in `default-ir-pipeline`.

 `simplify` is a real rewriter — the GHC-style simplifier
 (`IR/Simplify.codex`) — that folds constants, propagates literals, and
 takes the known arm of a decided `if`, iterated to a fixpoint. It
 returns the rewritten chapter AND, on the info channel, one line with
 its rewrite count, so `-Passes simplify` both transforms the program
 and reports how much it did. It is opt-in and NOT in
 `default-ir-pipeline`: it absorbs the rules of the hardcoded
 `fold-constants` pass but does not yet replace it, so the shipping
 compiler's output is unchanged until that swap lands as its own CL.

 `lir-dump` is on the analysis channel like `occ-report`: it lowers each
 definition to the flat LIR (`IR/Lir.codex`, step 5), runs the structural
 verifier, and reports one `LIR <def>: ...` line per definition — the dump
 plus the check result, or a note that the function was outside the
 whitelist. It rewrites nothing and emits a binary, so `-Passes lir-dump`
 drives the real lowering over real IR and reads the answer from the
 compile log, before any allocator consumes it. NOT in
 `default-ir-pipeline`.

 Unlike the other passes, `lir-dump` does NOT execute inside this
 pipeline: the constructor-pattern increment needs its scrutinee's
 resolved `SumTy`, and this pipeline runs before the RESOLVE phase. So the
 registry entry is inert (it returns nothing) and `opening.codex` produces
 the LIR report from `lir-report-if` after RESOLVE and LIFT, over the same
 `cdx-chapter` the emitter consumes. The pipeline name is still the
 trigger; only the execution site moved.

  default-ir-pipeline : List Text
  default-ir-pipeline = ["fold-constants", "inline-leaf-calls"]

  run-ir-pass : Text, IRChapter -> IRPassResult
  run-ir-pass (name) (ch) =
   if name == "fold-constants" then IRPassResult { chapter = fold-constants-in-chapter ch, violations = [], infos = [] }
   else if name == "inline-leaf-calls" then IRPassResult { chapter = inline-leaf-calls-in-chapter ch, violations = [], infos = [] }
   else if name == "ir-check" then IRPassResult { chapter = ch, violations = ir-check ch, infos = [] }
   else if name == "occ-report" then IRPassResult { chapter = ch, violations = [], infos = occ-report-chapter ch }
   else if name == "simplify" then let o = simplify-run ch in IRPassResult { chapter = o.chapter, violations = [], infos = o.infos }
   else if name == "lir-dump" then IRPassResult { chapter = ch, violations = [], infos = [] }
   else IRPassResult { chapter = ch, violations = [], infos = [] }

Section: Pipeline

 The chapter folds through the named passes in list order. The loop
 carries an index, the current chapter, and the violations reported so
 far; every allocation is the pass's own, on whatever deck the caller
 placed the run.

 Each violation is tagged with the slot that produced it, so a pipeline
 carrying `ir-check` twice says which of the two fired — that is the
 whole point of the exercise, attributing malformed IR to the pass that
 made it. A pass that reports nothing costs nothing here: the tagging
 loop sees an empty list and returns.

  run-ir-pipeline : List Text, IRChapter -> IRPassResult
  run-ir-pipeline (names) (ch) =
   run-ir-pipeline-loop names 0 (list-length names) ch [] []

  run-ir-pipeline-loop : List Text, Integer, Integer, IRChapter, List Text, List Text -> IRPassResult
  run-ir-pipeline-loop (names) (i) (len) (ch) (vs) (infos) =
   if i >= len then IRPassResult { chapter = ch, violations = vs, infos = infos }
   else let r = run-ir-pass (list-at names i) ch
   in let vs2 = tag-pass-violations vs i (r.violations) 0 (list-length (r.violations))
   in let infos2 = tag-pass-violations infos i (r.infos) 0 (list-length (r.infos))
   in run-ir-pipeline-loop names (i + 1) len (r.chapter) vs2 infos2

  tag-pass-violations : List Text, Integer, List Text, Integer, Integer -> List Text
  tag-pass-violations (acc) (slot) (vs) (i) (len) =
   if i >= len then acc
   else tag-pass-violations (list-push acc ("pass " & integer-to-text slot & ": " & (list-at vs i))) slot vs (i + 1) len

Section: Configuration

 The pipeline arrives on the mode line as `passes=name,name` — comma
 separated, no spaces — and the split lives here so the grammar sits
 beside the registry it names. The knob is the ablation surface: a
 pass that cannot be switched off cannot be measured, and the
 middle-end campaign scores pipeline configurations against each
 other rather than trusting any pass on reputation.

  parse-pipeline-names : Text -> List Text
  parse-pipeline-names (names) = text-split names ","
